## Conclusion

The group should not search for the market with the easiest definition of “green.” It should build a financing platform capable of surviving scrutiny across all target markets.

The recommended model combines:

1. a global green-finance framework with conservative minimum standards;
2. separate EU, Gulf, and Asian project pools and issuance supplements;
3. project-level taxonomy crosswalks rather than claims of universal alignment;
4. green bonds only for substantiated eligible expenditures;
5. transition, sustainability-linked, blended, or conventional finance for other decarbonization needs;
6. a formal measurement-quality grading system;
7. staged eligibility for plants with weak emissions data;
8. independent technical review and external assurance;
9. transparent reporting of taxonomy differences, underperformance, and uncertainty;
10. strict controls against double allocation and double counting; and
11. predefined suspension, reallocation, and correction procedures.

The objective is not to maximize labeled issuance in the first year. It is to become a credible repeat issuer whose environmental claims, project records, and impact data remain defensible even when taxonomies, ratings, and investor expectations diverge.
